<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Derbforgaill</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1108–<a href="/years/1193">1193</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Dervorgilla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Derbforgaill was an Irish noblewoman, daughter of Murchad Ua Máel Sechlainn, king of Mide, and wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigernan-ua-ruairc">Tigernán Ua Ruairc</a>, king of Breifne. In 1152 she was carried off, apparently willingly, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a> of Leinster. The resulting feud contributed to Diarmait's expulsion from Ireland in 1166 and his appeal to Henry II, which led to the Anglo-Norman invasion. She later retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellifont-abbey">Mellifont Abbey</a>, where she died.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Derbforgaill's mother was Mor, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-ua-briain">Muirchertach Ua Briain</a>, who died in 1137. Unusually for a woman of her time, she is named five times in the contemporary annals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigernan-ua-ruairc">Tigernán Ua Ruairc</a> had three children, Melaghlin, Aed and Dowchawley, the last the wife of the high king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruaidri-ua-conchobair">Ruaidri Ua Conchobair</a>, but whether Derbforgaill was their mother is uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her abduction in 1152 took place during a joint campaign against her husband by Tairdelbach Ua Conchobair, king of Ireland, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, which burned Bun Cuilinn and defeated Tigernán. The Annals of Tigernach, probably the only contemporary account, say Diarmait carried her out of Meath with her wealth; the Annals of the Four Masters add her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and furniture and that he acted on the advice of her brother Maeleachlainn. By the end of 1153 she had left Leinster for her family's lands in Meath, possibly after negotiations with her father's family.</p><p>Most historians see dynastic politics rather than romance in the affair, perhaps an attempt by the royal family of Meath to forge an alliance with Diarmait through marriage. Later English writers made more of it. Gerald of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>'s Expugnatio Hibernica and the Anglo-Norman poem The Song of Dermot and the Earl run the abduction of 1152 together with Diarmait's banishment of 1166, and Gerald blames her, writing that she was abducted because she wanted to be. Tigernán held the grudge: in 1167 he claimed 100 ounces of gold in compensation from Diarmait, enforced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruaidri-ua-conchobair">Ruaidri Ua Conchobair</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Derbforgaill was a patron of the church. At the consecration of the Cistercian abbey of Mellifont in 1157 she gave altar cloths, a gold chalice and 60 ounces of gold, and in 1167 she completed the Nuns' Church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonmacnoise">Clonmacnoise</a>. She died in 1193. Known as the Helen of Ireland, she has been given a part in bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a> to Ireland that historians regard as often greatly exaggerated and misinterpreted.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Derbforgaill ingen Maeleachlainn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tigernan Ua Ruairc, ruler from about 1124 to 1172, fought Diarmait Mac Murchada of Leinster, who had abducted his wife <b>Derbforgaill</b>; their feud helped bring the Normans to Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-breifne">Kingdom of Breifne</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in County Meath, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonmacnoise">Clonmacnoise</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.67,-6.67&amp;year=1163" aria-label="County Meath on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Derbforgaill"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4663 5524h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4663 5524h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.67,-6.67&amp;year=1163">What was here in 1163? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
